Variables in Ansible

Today I learned about Variables in Ansible, which make automation flexible and reusable instead of hardcoding values.
Instead of writing the same values again and again, variables help us write smarter and dynamic Playbooks ⚡

🔹 🧩 vars (Inline Variables)
The vars keyword is used to define variables directly inside a Playbook.
Example:

vars:
 package_name: nginx

These variables can be reused in tasks using {{ variable_name }}.
 It makes Playbooks cleaner and easier to manage.

🔹 📂 vars_files
vars_files allows us to store variables in a separate file and import them into the Playbook.
Example:

vars_files:
 - vars.yml

Benefits:
 ✔ Better organization
 ✔ Reusability
 ✔ Clean Playbook structure
This is very useful in real production environments.

🔹 🖥️ host_vars & group_vars
Ansible allows defining variables based on hosts and groups.
📌 host_vars → Variables specific to a single host
 📌 group_vars → Variables applied to a group of hosts
Example use cases:
 Different IPs for different servers
 Different configurations for dev & prod environments
This makes automation more environment-specific 🌍

🔹 🏆 Variable Precedence
Variable precedence decides which variable value Ansible will use if the same variable is defined in multiple places.

Order (high to low priority concept):
 Extra vars > Playbook vars > host_vars/group_vars > defaults

Higher precedence variables override lower ones.
 Understanding this prevents unexpected configuration issues ⚠️

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Variables make Playbooks dynamic.
 host_vars & group_vars enable environment-specific automation.
 Correct precedence = predictable automation.
